Assumption <a href="#hyp:campana" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="hyp:campana">4</a> implies the following statement. Let $f:M\to S$ be a fibration between smooth connected complex projective varieties, and let $D_M,D_S$ be reduced effective simple normal crossing divisors, possibly zero, such that $$\label{red:boundary-compatibility}
 \mathop{\mathrm{Supp}}(f^*D_S)\subseteq\mathop{\mathrm{Supp}}(D_M).$$ For a very general smooth fiber $F$, with $D_F=D_M|_F$, one has $$\label{red:log-iitaka-inequality}
 \kappa(M,K_M+D_M)
 \geq \kappa(F,K_F+D_F)+\kappa(S,K_S+D_S).$$ The usual convention for a $-\infty$ summand applies.

Let $(Z,\Delta)$ be an ordinary rational klt pair on a normal $n$-dimensional compact Kähler space, with actual $\mathbb{Q}$-Cartier adjoint $J=K_Z+\Delta$. Let $\pi:Z\to T$ be a projective bimeromorphic morphism with connected fibers to a normal compact Kähler space. Assume that $-J$ is relatively ample and that all contracted curves have class in one $J$-negative ray $R$ of $\overline{\mathrm{NA}}(Z)$. Let $k>0$ be an integer such that $kJ$ is Cartier, and let $L$ be a line bundle with $L\cdot R>0$. For any nontrivial fiber $F$ of $\pi$, $$\label{pos:index-bound}
 r_F:=\sup\{u:L+uJ\text{ has nonnegative degree on all curves of }F\}
 \geq\frac{1}{k(n+1)}.$$ No local analytic $\mathbb{Q}$-factoriality is required. In particular, this applies to the contractions in Assumption <a href="#hyp:mmp" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="hyp:mmp">5</a>, with $n=4$.

For every closed positive $(1,1)$-current $T$ in the class $\alpha$, one has $$\label{fol:annihilations}
 T\wedge\sigma\wedge\bar\sigma=0,\qquad
 T\wedge\beta_2\wedge\bar\beta_2=0$$ on the open subset where $p$ is an isomorphism onto a smooth open subset of $Y$. In particular, $$\label{fol:T-eta}
 T\wedge\boldsymbol\eta=0$$ on a dense open subset with analytic complement.

Let $T$ be a positive current in $\alpha$ with zero Lelong numbers everywhere. Choose local plurisubharmonic metric weights $\phi$ for $T$ on $L$. On the isomorphism locus of $p$, write $\boldsymbol\eta=\eta\otimes\tau$ in the corresponding local canonical frame $\tau$. The expression $$\label{fol:Theta-definition}
 \Theta_T=\sqrt{-1}e^{-\phi}\eta\wedge\bar\eta$$ has a canonical extension to a nonzero positive closed $(1,1)$-current on $M$. This extension has no mass on proper analytic subsets and satisfies $$\label{fol:Theta-orthogonality}
 \alpha\smile[\Theta_T]=0.$$ The weights are understood with a fixed global additive normalization when $\Theta_T$ is compared for different currents.

There is a positive current $T\in\alpha$ such that, on all the smooth index charts constructed in Lemma <a href="#fol:transverse-current" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="fol:transverse-current">24</a>, $$\label{fol:fixed-point-equation}
 \sqrt{-1}\partial\bar\partial\phi
 =\sqrt{-1}b e^{-\phi}\eta\wedge\bar\eta$$ for one constant $b>0$. On these charts $\phi$ is smooth and $$\label{fol:eta-derivative}
 \mathrm{d}\eta=\partial\phi\wedge\eta.$$

Let $M$ be a compatible system of mixed Hodge modules in perverse degree zero on the scheme etale charts of $\mathcal Y$. Suppose its support is finite over $\mathcal P$. Then, for every integer $k$, every positive integer $b$, and every $j<0$, $$\mathbb H^j\!\left(
   \mathcal Y,\mathop{\mathrm{Gr}}^F_k\mathop{\mathrm{DR}}_{\mathcal Y}(M)\otimes p^*C^{-b}
 \right)=0.$$ Here the graded de Rham complex is the descended coherent complex.

Let $P$ be nef, semiample, and big on a projective $d$-fold $Z$. Work at very general smooth marked points $x$. Choose $d+1$ levels $$\tau_0<\tau_1<\cdots<\tau_d,\qquad
 \tau_{i+1}-\tau_i=\delta>0.$$ Suppose, in sufficiently large divisible degree $k$, all the spaces $${\cal V}_{i,x}
 =H^0\bigl(Z,kP\otimes\mathfrak m_x^{\lceil k\tau_i\rceil}\bigr)$$ are nonzero, and the base locus of ${\cal V}_{0,x}$ has a positive-dimensional component through $x$. Then one obtains an algebraic family of integral components $V=V_x$ sweeping $Z$, of dimension $0<f<d$, and a step for which $V$ is a component of both successive base loci. At its generic point the higher subseries has an isolated base component $V$ and vanishes to order at least $k\delta/2$, once $k$ is sufficiently large. Moreover $$\label{proj:jet:tracking-estimates}
 \begin{split}
  P^f.V&\le (2/\delta)^{d-f}P^d,\\
  K_{V^*}P^{f-1}
   &\le (K_Z+cP)|_V P^{f-1},
       \qquad 0<c\le 2d/\delta,
 \end{split}$$ whenever $Z$ is $\mathbb{Q}$-factorial klt.

Both the total class $M$ and the slice class have big semiample positive parts $P$ on $\mathbb{Q}$-factorial klt models $Z$, with $$K_Z+\Delta\sim_{\mathbb{Q}}c_tP,\qquad \Delta\ge0,$$ where $c_t$ is bounded independently of small $t$. Their volumes satisfy, respectively, $$\begin{aligned}
 \mathop{\mathrm{vol}}(M)&=\frac{(2n+1)!}{(n!)^2}
   \int_0^q\mathop{\mathrm{vol}}(L_b)\mathop{\mathrm{vol}}(L_{q-b})\,db,\label{proj:jet:total-volume}\\
 \mathop{\mathrm{vol}}(L+q\xi)&=(n+1)\int_0^q\mathop{\mathrm{vol}}(L_b)\,db.\label{proj:jet:slice-volume}
\end{aligned}$$ In particular both volumes are bounded above and below by positive constants times $q$. For each rational weight, on common resolutions, $$\label{proj:jet:weight-domination}
 P\sim_{\mathbb{Q}}N_{b,1}+N_{q-b,2}+E_b,\qquad E_b\ge0,$$ with the analogous one-term formula on slices. At a generic point of a base divisor of a slice, and on a general torus fiber, the full system has no fixed subtraction.

For the bundles in Equation <a href="#proj:fr:graded-pieces" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="proj:fr:graded-pieces">[proj:fr:graded-pieces]</a>, $$h^0(W,\mathcal G_j)
      \leq e_jp^n\bigl(7^nH^n+o(1)\bigr),$$ uniformly in $j$. Consequently, for all sufficiently large $p$, $$\label{proj:fr:diagonal-rank}
 h^0(\Delta_F,\mathcal L_p)\leq\frac{s^2}{4}.$$
